{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "Severe trauma is a major global public health issue [1]. According to the World Health Organization, the three most common causes of injury and violence-related deaths specifically road traffic accidents, suicides, and homicides. Furthermore, road traffic crashes and falls are the main causes of traumatic brain injury (TBI) [2]; intracranial bleeding is a possible complication of TBI [3], whose frequency depends on the injury severity [4]. Severely injured trauma patients are characterized by coagulation abnormalities and a substantially increased mortality rate [5]. Approximately 33% patients with TBI present with coagulopathy, which can increase the hemorrhage expansion and risk of death [4]. Hemorrhagic death generally occurs within the first few hours of injury (median time to hemorrhagic death is 2-2.6 h [6]) ... Early detection and management of traumatic hemorrhage and TBI with intracranial hematoma can improve clinical outcomes [10], and, as suggested by the European guidelines, 'current guidelines are needed for the implementation of evidence-based local protocols and algorithms together with parameters to assess key measures of bleeding control and outcome' [1, 9].",
      "reason": "The Introduction describes the current state of knowledge about trauma, TBI, coagulopathy, timing of hemorrhagic death, and need for guideline-driven management, highlighting important uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Purpose The effect of systemic hemostatic agents initiated during pre-hospital care of severely injured patients with ongoing bleeding or traumatic brain injury (TBI) remains controversial. A systematic review and meta-analysis was therefore conducted to assess the effectiveness and safety of systemic hemostatic agents as an adjunctive therapy in people with major trauma and hemorrhage or TBI in the context of developing the Italian National Institute of Health guidelines on major trauma integrated management.",
      "reason": "Abstract explicitly states the controversy and that the review was conducted to assess effectiveness/safety and to inform national guideline development, explaining the importance and need for the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_21",
      "quote": "To our knowledge, this is the first comprehensive systematic review with a meta-analysis assessing the certainty of evidence by the GRADE approach to prove the efficacy of systemic hemostatic agents as an adjunctive treatment initiated in the pre-hospital setting among traumatic patients with (1) hemorrhage, (2) TBI, or (3) hemorrhage and TBI.",
      "reason": "The manuscript identifies prior reviews in the evidence and explains the novelty/need for the current review (first comprehensive review using GRADE and meta-analysis in this specific pre-hospital context)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_21",
      "quote": "The effect of systemic hemostatic agents depends on the time between injury and the onset of treatment, which suggests there is a protective effect of these medications on early deaths related to hemorrhage [48]. The treatment benefit is time-dependent, the benefits of TXA therapy seen in the first 3 h may be explained by the increasing levels of plasminogen activator inhibitor-1 and a reduction in fibrinolysis. In CRASH-2, when TXA was administered within 3 h of injury, the risk of death due to bleeding was reduced [29].",
      "reason": "The text describes a proposed mechanism and time-dependent action for TXA (effects on fibrinolysis and plasminogen activator inhibitor-1) explaining how the intervention may work."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence was found in the retrieved text of a logic model or schematic showing hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes for complex interventions or delivery contexts."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

